1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,465
A mowed lawn.

2
00:00:01,465 --> 00:00:02,386
A root tip.

3
00:00:02,386 --> 00:00:04,040
A new branch on a tree.

4
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:07,075
The college lawn was cut short on Monday.

5
00:00:07,075 --> 00:00:11,663
By Friday, the grass was tall again,
even though its tips were cut off.

6
00:00:12,113 --> 00:00:14,269
Diya noticed it on her way to class.

7
00:00:14,269 --> 00:00:18,409
She wants to be a doctor, and she
asked: if the tips are gone, where is

8
00:00:18,409 --> 00:00:19,807
the grass growing from?

9
00:00:20,157 --> 00:00:23,710
The answer is a special tissue
whose cells keep dividing.

10
00:00:23,710 --> 00:00:25,390
It is called a meristem.

11
00:00:25,890 --> 00:00:30,171
By the end, you will find the three
kinds of meristem in a plant, and say

12
00:00:30,171 --> 00:00:31,527
what each one builds.

13
00:00:32,177 --> 00:00:33,800
First, what is a tissue?

14
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:38,864
A tissue is a group of cells with a
common origin, usually doing a common

15
00:00:38,864 --> 00:00:39,764
job.

16
00:00:39,826 --> 00:00:44,596
In plants, growth happens mostly in
special spots, where cells divide actively.

17
00:00:44,596 --> 00:00:46,646
These spots are called meristems.

18
00:00:47,096 --> 00:00:50,597
The word comes from the Greek
meristos, which means divided.

19
00:00:50,597 --> 00:00:52,822
Meristem cells divide again and again.

20
00:00:53,472 --> 00:00:56,791
Let's zoom into a plant,
one level at a time.

21
00:00:57,141 --> 00:01:01,550
A plant grows longer at two tips:
the tip of the shoot, and the tip of

22
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:02,450
the root.

23
00:01:02,817 --> 00:01:04,582
Zoom into the shoot tip.

24
00:01:04,582 --> 00:01:07,750
At the very top sits the
shoot apical meristem.

25
00:01:07,750 --> 00:01:10,241
Apical means at the apex, the tip.

26
00:01:10,241 --> 00:01:13,761
The bumps beside it are
leaf primordia, baby leaves.

27
00:01:14,261 --> 00:01:15,540
Now the root tip.

28
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:18,974
The root apical meristem sits
just behind the root cap.

29
00:01:18,974 --> 00:01:22,656
The cap protects it, as the
root pushes through the soil.

30
00:01:23,156 --> 00:01:25,236
Zoom into the meristem cells.

31
00:01:25,236 --> 00:01:30,041
They are small, with thin walls,
dense cytoplasm and a large nucleus.

32
00:01:30,041 --> 00:01:32,132
Their main job is to divide.

33
00:01:32,782 --> 00:01:36,463
Now watch what a meristem
does, one cell at a time.

34
00:01:36,813 --> 00:01:37,713
Step one.

35
00:01:37,531 --> 00:01:41,408
A meristem cell grows a little,
and copies what is in its nucleus.

36
00:01:42,158 --> 00:01:43,058
Step two.

37
00:01:42,757 --> 00:01:44,296
It divides into two new cells.

38
00:01:45,046 --> 00:01:45,946
Step three.

39
00:01:45,913 --> 00:01:47,797
This happens again and again.

40
00:01:47,797 --> 00:01:51,396
New cells pile up behind the
tip, and the tip moves on.

41
00:01:52,146 --> 00:01:53,046
Step four.

42
00:01:52,899 --> 00:01:55,791
The cells left behind grow
bigger and change shape.

43
00:01:55,791 --> 00:01:58,899
Each becomes special for one
job, like carrying water.

44
00:01:59,649 --> 00:02:00,549
Step five.

45
00:02:00,465 --> 00:02:02,687
Now they lose the power to divide.

46
00:02:02,687 --> 00:02:06,869
They are called permanent cells,
and they form the permanent tissues.

47
00:02:07,619 --> 00:02:09,796
Plants have three kinds of meristem.

48
00:02:09,796 --> 00:02:11,748
Each sits in a different place.

49
00:02:12,098 --> 00:02:14,937
Apical meristems sit at the
tips of roots and shoots.

50
00:02:14,937 --> 00:02:16,479
They make the plant longer.

51
00:02:16,979 --> 00:02:19,555
Intercalary meristems sit
between mature tissues.

52
00:02:19,555 --> 00:02:23,362
They occur in grasses, and regrow
the parts that grazing animals eat.

53
00:02:23,362 --> 00:02:25,315
That is how Diya's lawn grew back.

54
00:02:25,815 --> 00:02:29,727
Lateral meristems sit along the sides
of older roots and stems, shaped

55
00:02:29,727 --> 00:02:31,195
like a hollow cylinder.

56
00:02:31,195 --> 00:02:33,075
They make woody plants thicker.

57
00:02:33,575 --> 00:02:38,166
And as the shoot grows, some cells
are left behind by the shoot apical

58
00:02:38,166 --> 00:02:39,066
meristem.

59
00:02:38,943 --> 00:02:43,686
They form the axillary bud, in the
angle between a leaf and the stem.

60
00:02:43,686 --> 00:02:46,590
It can grow into a branch, or a flower.

61
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:48,147
Side by side.

62
00:02:48,147 --> 00:02:49,221
When they appear.

63
00:02:49,221 --> 00:02:52,931
Apical and intercalary meristems
appear early in the life of a plant.

64
00:02:52,931 --> 00:02:54,476
They are primary meristems.

65
00:02:54,476 --> 00:02:57,095
Lateral meristems appear
later, in older parts.

66
00:02:57,095 --> 00:02:58,723
They are secondary meristems.

67
00:02:59,473 --> 00:03:00,543
What they build.

68
00:03:00,543 --> 00:03:03,170
Primary meristems build
the primary plant body.

69
00:03:03,170 --> 00:03:07,128
Secondary meristems build secondary
tissues, and make the plant thicker.

70
00:03:07,878 --> 00:03:08,778
Examples.

71
00:03:08,552 --> 00:03:12,502
Root and shoot apical meristems, and
the intercalary meristem in grass,

72
00:03:12,502 --> 00:03:13,402
are primary.

73
00:03:13,362 --> 00:03:17,259
The fascicular vascular cambium, the
interfascicular cambium and the cork

74
00:03:17,259 --> 00:03:19,341
cambium are lateral, or secondary.

75
00:03:20,091 --> 00:03:20,991
Your turn.

76
00:03:20,901 --> 00:03:25,275
A cow eats the top of the grass,
yet the blades grow back from below.

77
00:03:25,275 --> 00:03:26,848
Which meristem did that?

78
00:03:26,848 --> 00:03:28,039
Pause and decide.

79
00:03:31,239 --> 00:03:32,139
Intercalary!

80
00:03:32,125 --> 00:03:36,585
It sits between mature tissues, lower
down, so it survives when the top

81
00:03:36,585 --> 00:03:37,485
is eaten.

82
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:39,022
Remember this.

83
00:03:39,022 --> 00:03:40,686
Tips make a plant longer.

84
00:03:40,686 --> 00:03:42,155
Sides make it thicker.

85
00:03:42,755 --> 00:03:44,174
Now, a worked example.

86
00:03:44,174 --> 00:03:45,074
Six meristems.

87
00:03:45,073 --> 00:03:46,315
Primary or secondary?

88
00:03:46,665 --> 00:03:49,615
The root apical meristem, at the root tip.

89
00:03:49,615 --> 00:03:50,515
Primary.

90
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:53,581
The cork cambium, in an old tree trunk.

91
00:03:53,581 --> 00:03:54,481
Secondary.

92
00:03:54,847 --> 00:03:57,795
The shoot apical meristem,
at the shoot tip.

93
00:03:57,795 --> 00:03:58,695
Primary.

94
00:03:58,936 --> 00:04:00,142
The interfascicular cambium.

95
00:04:00,142 --> 00:04:01,042
Secondary.

96
00:04:01,126 --> 00:04:03,649
The intercalary meristem in grass.

97
00:04:03,649 --> 00:04:04,549
Primary.

98
00:04:04,895 --> 00:04:07,486
And the fascicular vascular cambium.

99
00:04:07,486 --> 00:04:08,386
Secondary.

100
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:10,491
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:10,841 --> 00:04:15,305
Many students think the intercalary
meristem is a secondary meristem, because

102
00:04:15,305 --> 00:04:16,799
it is not at a tip.

103
00:04:17,199 --> 00:04:21,656
But it appears early, and builds the
primary body, just like the apical

104
00:04:21,656 --> 00:04:22,556
meristems.

105
00:04:22,444 --> 00:04:24,633
Intercalary is a primary meristem.

106
00:04:25,283 --> 00:04:26,762
Where is this asked?

107
00:04:26,762 --> 00:04:31,317
In ISC papers, as the location and
role of each meristem, or as naming

108
00:04:31,317 --> 00:04:32,230
an example.

109
00:04:32,830 --> 00:04:33,961
Exam level now.

110
00:04:33,961 --> 00:04:36,177
Read the assertion and the reason.

111
00:04:36,177 --> 00:04:37,357
Pause and solve.

112
00:04:42,757 --> 00:04:44,022
The answer is A.

113
00:04:44,522 --> 00:04:48,931
Grasses do regrow after grazing, and
they do have intercalary meristems.

114
00:04:49,431 --> 00:04:52,964
The intercalary meristem is what
regrows the eaten part, so the reason

115
00:04:52,964 --> 00:04:54,241
explains the assertion.

116
00:04:54,841 --> 00:04:56,381
Here is your recap.

117
00:04:56,381 --> 00:04:57,964
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:01,164 --> 00:05:03,350
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:04,650
One.

120
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:06,556
Where does the root apical meristem sit?

121
00:05:07,456 --> 00:05:08,356
Two.

122
00:05:07,750 --> 00:05:08,811
Name two lateral meristems.

123
00:05:09,711 --> 00:05:10,611
Three.

124
00:05:10,181 --> 00:05:12,904
Why are apical and intercalary
meristems called primary?

125
00:05:14,004 --> 00:05:18,688
Next time: why is a coconut husk
so tough, and a potato so soft?

126
00:05:18,688 --> 00:05:22,225
We find out in simple and
complex permanent tissues.
